<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Constans I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 323–<a href="/years/350">350</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Constans I was a Roman emperor, the youngest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a>, who ruled from 337 to 350. After defeating his brother Constantine II in 340 he controlled the whole western empire, campaigning against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> in 341–342 and visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 343. He was overthrown by the usurper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnentius">Magnentius</a> and killed in 350 while fleeing towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constans-i.jpg" alt="Constans I" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ladislav Luppa · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mus%C3%A9e_du_Louvre_-_L%27empereur_Constant_(Ma_1021).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Constans was the third and youngest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a> and Fausta. He was educated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> under the poet Aemilius Magnus Arborius, who taught him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. On 25 December 333 his father raised him to caesar. He was engaged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympias</a>, daughter of the praetorian prefect Ablabius, but never married.</p><p>After Constantine's death, Constans and his brothers Constantine II and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-ii">Constantius II</a> were proclaimed augusti and divided the empire on 9 September 337. Constans received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and Illyricum, and in 338 he campaigned against the Sarmatians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Constantine II tried to issue legislation for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> in 339 and invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> a year later, but was ambushed and killed by Constans' troops, and Constans took over his territories. From 341 to 342 he campaigned against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, after an initial setback ending in victory and a favourable peace. In early 343 he visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, for reasons that remain unclear.</p><p>He issued an edict in 341 banning pagan sacrifices but soon legislated against the destruction of temples. His support for Nicene orthodoxy and Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athanasius-of-alexandria">Athanasius of Alexandria</a> brought him into conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-ii">Constantius II</a>. The Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serdica">Serdica</a> of 343 failed to settle it, and by 345 Constans was threatening civil war, until Constantius let Athanasius return. He also used the army against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donatism">Donatism</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 18 January 350 the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnentius">Magnentius</a> was proclaimed emperor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/autun">Augustodunum</a> (Autun) with the support of court officials such as Marcellinus. Constans, on a hunting trip, tried to reach <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> but was cornered at Helena (Elne) in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> and killed after seeking sanctuary in a temple.</p><p>Ancient sources, which may reflect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnentius">Magnentius</a>' propaganda, blame his fall on corruption, neglect of parts of the empire and contempt for soldiers. Jill Harries and others doubt this and see the revolt as the work of an apprehensive official, noting that Constans often travelled with only a small escort.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Constans” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The emperors answered with punishing campaigns: Constantine the Great threw the captured Frankish kings Ascaric and Merogaisus to wild beasts in the arena at Trier around 306, and <b>Constans</b> fought the Franks in 341–342.</blockquote><a href="/empire/franks">Franks · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Elne, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.5,12.5&amp;year=340" aria-label="Italy on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Constans I"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5782 3897h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5782 3897h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.5,12.5&amp;year=340">What was here in 340 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
